# November 19, 2026: a first scatter of places across New Brunswick, from Edmundston and Miscou in the north to Grand Manan
# and Saint John in the south, Hartland and McAdam in the west and Port Elgin and Memramcook in the east. Region nb was
# drawn by tools/choose.py --claim (the scheduled run asks for the thinnest province; nb had one place, Fort Beausejour).
# Sources opened in this session: the Canadian Register of Historic Places statement for every place (historicplaces.ca,
# read with tools/page.py --all; the character-defining elements say what survives); Wikipedia as raw text
# (tools/wiki_raw.py); Parks Canada's own pages (Carleton Martello Tower, St. Andrews Blockhouse, Fort Gaspareaux,
# Monument-Lefebvre, Boishebert and Beaubears Island); Kings Landing's, the Village Historique Acadien's and Ministers
# Island's own pages; the Government of New Brunswick's Government House page; the Anglican Diocese of Fredericton's page
# and the cathedral's own tour notice for Christ Church Cathedral; the University of New Brunswick's Military Heritage
# Project page for the Petit-Sault blockhouse; Lighthouse Friends for Miscou.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
def HP(t,i): return ['Canadian Register of Historic Places: '+t,'https://www.historicplaces.ca/en/rep-reg/place-lieu.aspx?id=%d'%i]
BATCH={'key':'nb_tqwk','label':'New Brunswick: a first scatter','added':'2026-11-19 18:58'}
PLACES=[
# ---- The upper Saint John valley and the west ----
N('ptit-sault-blockhouse','Petit-Sault Blockhouse (Fortin du Petit-Sault)','Edmundston','nb','fort','standing',47.36362,-68.32207,'site','view','walk','forts','1841',
  'The British built a blockhouse on this rock at the meeting of the Madawaska and Saint John rivers in 1841, during the bloodless Aroostook War over where the border between New Brunswick and Maine ran; the war ended with the Webster-Ashburton Treaty in 1842, which split the Madawaska settlements between the two. It had three floors, the first of stone and the upper two of heavy logs, inside a palisade, with room for an officer and 50 men. Lightning destroyed it in August 1855 (the Register says it burned in 1855).',
  'A reconstruction of the blockhouse on the original site, opened to the public in 2001, on a rocky hillock about 150 feet above the two rivers, with views across to Maine. The Register lists the original foundation as an archaeological feature, and a provincial plaque. Interior and opening times not checked.',
  'The border fort of a war in which nobody was killed.',
  'A Provincial Historic Site at 10-14 St-Jean Avenue in Edmundston. The University of New Brunswick\'s Military Heritage Project page says the rebuilt blockhouse was opened to the public in 2001, but no page of whoever runs it was read, so opening times are not checked: look from the street until they are. Parking not checked: the lot OpenStreetMap maps nearest, on Rue Queen, looked like a business\'s on the aerial.',
  [HP("P'tit Sault Blockhouse",2156),L('New Brunswick Military Heritage Project: Petit Sault 1841 Blockhouse','https://www.unb.ca/nbmhp-database/madawaska-county/points-of-interest/petit-sault-1841-blockhouse-.php')],brick='?'),
N('hartland-covered-bridge','Hartland Covered Bridge','Hartland','nb','landmark','standing',46.29672,-67.53016,'exact','public','drive','','1901',
  'Opened in 1901 as an uncovered toll bridge across the Saint John River between Hartland and Somerville, framed as seven Howe-truss spans on six piers. In April 1920 a freshet and ice took out two spans on the west side; it was rebuilt from 1920 and covered, and a walkway was added in 1945. At 390.75 metres (1,282 feet) it is reckoned the longest covered bridge in the world, a National Historic Site since 1977.',
  'The whole bridge: Howe trusses in wood framing behind vertical weatherboarding under a gabled roof, on concrete piers and abutments, with the pedestrian walkway hung on its side, shingled arched portals at both ends, window openings along its length and electric lights inside.',
  'Drive the longest covered bridge in the world.',
  'A public road, Hartland Bridge Hill Road, still open to traffic: one lane, with a height limit and a load limit cut from ten tonnes to three after maintenance in 2023 (Wikipedia). Walk it on the side walkway. OpenStreetMap maps parking lots near both ends; which are public was not checked.',
  [W('Hartland Covered Bridge'),HP('Hartland Covered Bridge National Historic Site of Canada',7623)],brick='no'),
N('mcadam-railway-station','McAdam Railway Station','McAdam','nb','rail','standing',45.59003,-67.32885,'exact','open','drive','stations','1900–1994',
  'The Canadian Pacific built this combined station and hotel in 1900-01 at its junction for the line to St. Andrews, where wealthy passengers changed for the Algonquin hotel, and enlarged it in 1910-11. The 20-room hotel took up two thirds of the upper floor; it closed in the early 1960s. The CPR used the station until 1978 and VIA Rail until its Atlantic train was dropped on December 17, 1994. It is a National Historic Site, a rare Chateau-style station and one of the few that was also a hotel.',
  'A long two-and-a-half-storey station of local grey granite, rough-faced coursed blocks below the platform eaves and rubble above, trimmed with red Welsford granite at the windows and corners, under steep hipped roofs with bellcast eaves, stone-gabled dormers and a pointed tower; wide platform eaves on big timber brackets and the stationmaster\'s bay. Inside, a museum run by the McAdam Historical Restoration Commission, with railway pies served again at the lunch counter on Sundays since 2023 (Wikipedia). Interior fittings not checked.',
  'The village station built like a chateau, with a hotel upstairs.',
  'Run by the McAdam Historical Restoration Commission at 146 Saunders Road. Wikipedia says it opens for tours in the summer months; its own site (mcadamstation.ca) answered 403 here, so hours and booking are not checked. No parking lot is mapped; look for parking along Saunders Road.',
  [W('McAdam station'),HP('McAdam Railway Station (Canadian Pacific) National Historic Site of Canada',3207),L('McAdam Railway Station (its own site)','https://mcadamstation.ca/')],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('kings-landing-nb','Kings Landing Historical Settlement','Prince William','nb','town','standing',45.87703,-66.97803,'site','open','walk','','1790s–1920s',
  'When the Mactaquac dam was to flood the middle Saint John valley from Mactaquac to Upper Woodstock in the late 1960s, the historic buildings of the Loyalist and immigrant settlements along it were moved here instead. The Register counts 37 historic buildings (34 restored, 3 rehabilitated) and 28 reconstructed ones on 53 hectares along the river, two more having come from Fredericton later. It is not a real village but a made one, each building set to a year in its history and worked by costumed interpreters.',
  'Loyalist houses with five-bay fronts and shingle siding, two timber-trade neoclassical houses, a two-storey fieldstone side-hill house, an Irish immigrant\'s square log cabin (the only one preserved in the province, says the Register), ox, horse and stock barns of about 1840 in hewn post and beam, and two Gothic Revival churches, one with a butternut interior. Horse and wagon rides; allow three to four hours (its page).',
  'The villages the dam would have drowned, moved up the hill.',
  'A provincial historic site run by Kings Landing at 5804 Route 102, Prince William, 20 minutes west of Fredericton. Admission in 2026: adult $19.55, family $60 (its page). When read in November 2026 it was closed for the season, reopening in November for Christmas events; summer hours were not on the page. Outdoor village with unpaved paths; wheelchairs and scooters lent at the Welcome Centre. Parking at the Welcome Centre (OpenStreetMap).',
  [W('Kings Landing Historical Settlement'),HP('Kings Landing Historical Settlement',6301),L('Kings Landing: hours and prices','https://kingslanding.nb.ca/hours-and-prices/'),L('Kings Landing: plan your visit','https://kingslanding.nb.ca/plan-your-visit')],th=[45.87575,-66.97917],thn='Kings Landing lot (OpenStreetMap)',brick='?',hours='?'),
# ---- Fredericton ----
N('marysville-cotton-mill','Marysville Cotton Mill','Fredericton','nb','industry','standing',45.97824,-66.58879,'exact','view','walk','','1883–1975',
  'Alexander "Boss" Gibson built this cotton mill in 1883-85 to designs by Lockwood, Greene and Company of Boston, the New England mill engineers, in a model village of brick workers\' houses he named Marysville after his wife and eldest daughter. By 1900 it was among the largest mills in Canada, built on the "slow-burning" plan with electric light and machinery driven by belts and shafting. It made cloth until the 1970s (Wikipedia gives 1975 for the closing) and was turned into provincial government offices, Marysville Place.',
  'A four-storey red-brick "insurance mill" of locally made brick on brick piers, with a flat-roofed central tower, rows of large multi-pane windows and decorative brickwork on the main front; beside it the two-storey brick dye house annex. Most of the brick workers\' duplexes round it still stand in the Marysville historic district, and the old rail line beside the mill is now a trail. Interior (offices) not checked.',
  'A company town\'s mill that now houses the civil service.',
  'Provincial government offices at 20 McGloin Street; see it from the streets round it. The houses of the district are private. OpenStreetMap maps a large lot just south of the mill off Fisher Avenue; whether visitors may use it was not checked.',
  [W('Marysville, New Brunswick'),HP('Marysville Cotton Mill National Historic Site of Canada',12664)],th=[45.97713,-66.58843],thn='Lot south of the mill, off Fisher Avenue (OpenStreetMap)',brick='yes'),
N('old-government-house-fredericton','Old Government House','Fredericton','nb','landmark','standing',45.96564,-66.65589,'exact','open','walk','','1826–',
  'A Palladian sandstone mansion designed by J. W. Woolford and built in 1826-28 for the governor of New Brunswick, after the first residence of 1787 burned. On April 7, 1866 Governor Arthur Gordon and Premier Albert J. Smith met here in an encounter that cleared the way for Confederation. Governors lived here until 1893; it was then a school for deaf students, a military barracks and a soldiers\' hospital, and the RCMP\'s J Division headquarters until 1988 (from 1932 says the Register, from 1934 the province\'s page). Restored, it reopened in 1999 and is again the lieutenant governor\'s residence.',
  'Two and a half storeys of local sandstone with semicircular end bays, a pedimented frontispiece and a semicircular portico, high chimneys and a hipped roof, set back on 4.5 hectares between Woodstock Road and the river. Inside: the original public rooms with surviving furnishings, wood- and plasterwork and Tuscan columns in the entry hall; restored drawing room, dining room, library and the original lieutenant governor\'s office, and exhibit rooms upstairs.',
  'Where a governor and a premier argued Canada into being.',
  'The lieutenant governor\'s residence, run by the Government of New Brunswick. Its page: free bilingual guided tours Monday to Friday, June 1 to September 30, 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; phone ahead to be sure of a tour, and groups book. The grounds are always open for walks and picnics. Tours start at the visitors\' entrance on the east side, where there is free parking. A New Year\'s Day levee is open to the public.',
  [W('Government House (New Brunswick)'),HP('Old Government House National Historic Site of Canada',7642),L('Government of New Brunswick: Government House','https://www.gnb.ca/en/org/lieutenant-governor/government-house.html')],brick='no',hours='Guided tours Monday to Friday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m., June 1 to September 30; grounds always open'),
N('christ-church-cathedral-fredericton','Christ Church Cathedral','Fredericton','nb','worship','standing',45.95731,-66.6348,'exact','open','walk','','1845–1853',
  'Bishop John Medley, the first Anglican bishop of New Brunswick and a member of the Ecclesiological Society, had Frank Wills draw a cathedral after a 14th-century church in Norfolk, England; it was built from 1845 to 1853, with William Butterfield changing the east end to a single tower and designing much of the first furniture and plate. After a fire in 1911 J. deLancey Robinson of New York restored it in 1911-13, lengthening the spire. It became a pattern for Gothic Revival churches across Canada.',
  'Stone walls, a crossing tower and tall spire, steep roofs, pointed windows with elaborate tracery, buttresses, niches and finials, on a green by the Saint John River near the Bill Thorpe walking bridge. Inside, interior fittings not checked beyond the Register\'s note that its arrangement follows ecclesiological principles.',
  'The church that taught Canada to build Gothic.',
  'An active Anglican cathedral, 150 Church Street. Services (diocese\'s page): Sunday 8 and 10:30 a.m., Wednesday 10 a.m., Morning Prayer weekdays 8:45 a.m. Free guided tours daily in July and August (the cathedral\'s 2026 notice), with self-guided booklets in five languages. Parking not checked (the lot OpenStreetMap maps nearest fell on houses on the aerial); downtown street parking.',
  [W('Christ Church Cathedral (Fredericton)'),HP('Christ Church Cathedral National Historic Site of Canada',11974),L('Anglican Diocese of Fredericton: Christ Church Cathedral','https://nb.anglican.ca/fredericton/christ-church-cathedral'),L('Christ Church Cathedral: Tour the Cathedral','https://cccath.ca/2025/06/11/tour-the-cathedral-2025/amp')],brick='no',hours='Sunday 8 and 10:30 a.m.; Wednesday 10 a.m. July and August tours: Mon, Tue, Thu to Sat 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., Wed 11 to 5, Sun 1 to 4'),
# ---- Queens and Kings counties ----
N('reids-mill-smokestack','Reid\'s Mill smokestack','Gagetown','nb','industry','ruins',45.7622,-66.1404,'area','view','drive','','1919–1950s',
  'Richard and Frank Reid set up a big steam sawmill on the Saint John River just below the Gagetown ferry in 1919; it had a siding on the Valley Railway and shipped lumber by boxcar to the Boston area, and it was the village\'s largest employer. The mill burned in 1935, though the stack survived, and 600,000 board feet of lumber burned in 1941. Soon after the woods behind Gagetown were taken for Base Gagetown in 1953, the business, by then the Gagetown Lumber Company, closed and the mill was torn down.',
  'The one thing left: a stack about ninety feet tall, about six feet a side, of cast-in-place concrete girded with steel bars every five feet and lined with creamy-orange firebrick said to have come from Scotland, with a steel grating at the top where ospreys have nested; at its foot the larger block that held the furnace and boiler. A landmark from the river.',
  'All that a fire, another fire and an army base left of the mill.',
  'At 32 and 34 Old Mill Road, a Local Historic Place; who owns the ground was not checked, so look from Old Mill Road or from the river. No parking is mapped.',
  [HP("Reid's Mill Smokestack",15952)],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
# ---- Saint John and the Fundy shore ----
N('loyalist-house-saint-john','Loyalist House','Saint John','nb','landmark','standing',45.27452,-66.0613,'exact','open','walk','','1810–1817',
  'The merchant David Merritt, of a Loyalist family that landed at Parrtown from Rye, New York in 1783, built this house before 1820; five generations of Merritts lived in it, with few changes, until 1959. It survived the Great Fire of 1877 that destroyed much of the city and is one of the oldest houses in Saint John. The New Brunswick Historical Society opened it as a museum in 1961.',
  'A two-storey Federal-style house under a low hipped roof with four end chimneys, a five-bay front with a fanlit and sidelit centre door up a split stair, in clapboard and shingle; inside, finely made Adamesque mouldings and finishes and a centre-hall plan, furnished with Georgian and Victorian pieces from the Society, the New Brunswick Museum and Kings Landing (Wikipedia). Guided tours.',
  'A Loyalist merchant\'s house that the 1877 fire missed.',
  'A museum of the New Brunswick Historical Society at 120 Union Street. Wikipedia says it is open in business hours from late June to early September, and for cruise ships into October; its own site answered 403 here, so hours are not checked. OpenStreetMap maps the Peel Plaza parking garage about 200 m northwest.',
  [W('Loyalist House'),HP('Loyalist House National Historic Site of Canada',10691)],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('carleton-martello-tower','Carleton Martello Tower','Saint John','nb','fort','standing',45.25203,-66.07629,'exact','open','walk','forts','1813–1945',
  'A round stone tower begun in 1813 on a rocky height in West Saint John to guard the city\'s land approaches against an American invasion; it was finished only in 1815, after the war, and the guns planned for it were never mounted. It was improved in the 1830s and armed against the Fenians in the late 1860s, held deserters in the First World War, and in the Second carried a two-storey concrete command post on top as an observation and fire-control post for the harbour. A National Historic Site, open to the public since 1963.',
  'A squat two-storey tower of rubble masonry about 30 feet high and 50 across, its wall leaning slightly inward to the parapet, with shuttered windows and an arched door on the upper level reached by an outside stair, capped by the 1941 concrete command post. Inside: a restored powder magazine and barracks room and exhibits, and from the roof a view over the city and harbour (Wikipedia).',
  'A War of 1812 tower with a Second World War bunker on its head.',
  'Parks Canada, 454 Whipple Street. Open June 17 to October 11, 2026, Wednesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 5:30 p.m. (its page); fees on its page, free for youth. Parking, picnic area and lookout; dogs on a leash. Saint John Transit routes 15A and 15B stop a short walk away.',
  [W('Carleton Martello Tower'),HP('Carleton Martello Tower',9523),L('Parks Canada: Carleton Martello Tower','https://parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/nb/carleton'),L('Parks Canada: plan your visit','https://parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/nb/carleton/visit')],brick='no',hours='June 17 to October 11: Wednesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 5:30 p.m.'),
N('seal-cove-herring-stands','Seal Cove smoked herring stands','Grand Manan','nb','industry','standing',44.65181,-66.83738,'area','view','walk','','1870–1930',
  'Grand Manan became the world\'s largest supplier of smoked herring by 1884, and by 1920 it shipped a million boxes a year (Wikipedia). Seal Cove was a leading centre of the trade: round its cove, behind breakwaters, stand some 54 wooden buildings of the fishery, most built between 1870 and 1930, grouped into "stands" where herring were strung, smoked and boned. They were made a National Historic Site in 1995 as a rare survival of a once-common maritime landscape.',
  'Dense clusters of plain wooden buildings round the cove: shingled, gable-roofed stringing sheds on piers over the water, tall balloon-framed smokehouses with steep open-gable roofs and sliding windows on cement footings, boning sheds and small stores, pole wharves with their sluices and hoists, joined by narrow earth paths and lanes. How many still stand and still work was not checked.',
  'Fifty-odd smokehouses from the days when Grand Manan fed the world herring.',
  'Working fish premises on private ground in the village of Seal Cove; look from the public roads and the breakwater. The nearest mapped lot is on Seal Cove East Breakwater Road (OpenStreetMap).',
  [W('Grand Manan'),HP('Seal Cove Smoked Herring Stands National Historic Site of Canada',2343)],th=[44.65187,-66.83652],thn='Lot on Seal Cove East Breakwater Road (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no'),
# ---- Passamaquoddy Bay ----
N('st-andrews-blockhouse','St. Andrews Blockhouse','Saint Andrews','nb','fort','standing',45.0771,-67.06193,'exact','open','walk','forts','1813',
  'One of three blockhouses that the people of St. Andrews paid for and built, with militia help, against American privateers in the War of 1812; the Register calls it the only privately financed fortification of that war. It saw no action and served as a barracks, and was used again for defence in the 1860s. It is the last blockhouse of the War of 1812 left in Canada.',
  'A square timber blockhouse with a pyramid roof, its walls of 12-inch squared timbers laid flat and dovetailed at the corners and shingled outside; the upper storey overhangs the lower, with machicolation loopholes in the overhang, a gunport in the centre of each upper wall and musket holes in the ground floor; one door, and an open undivided room inside. It stands in a small park on the waterfront.',
  'The townspeople\'s own fort.',
  'Parks Canada, 23 Joes Point Road. Open June 2 to August 31, 2026, Tuesday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. (its page); fees on its page, free for youth. A picnic area, and the tidal flats can be walked at low tide. Mapped parking on Joes Point Road beside it (OpenStreetMap).',
  [W('St. Andrews Blockhouse'),HP('St. Andrews Blockhouse',7656),L('Parks Canada: St. Andrews Blockhouse','https://parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/nb/standrews')],th=[45.07741,-67.06238],thn='Lot on Joes Point Road (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no',hours='June 2 to August 31: Tuesday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
N('ministers-island-covenhoven','Ministers Island (Van Horne estate, Covenhoven)','Saint Andrews','nb','inst','standing',45.09851,-67.03393,'site','open','drive','','1890–1940s',
  'An island in Passamaquoddy Bay (200 hectares says Wikipedia, 280 the Register), named for the Reverend Samuel Andrews, who bought it in 1791. From 1890 Sir William Van Horne, president of the Canadian Pacific Railway, made it his summer estate and gentleman\'s farm, with buildings by the Montreal architect Edward Maxwell; his family kept it into the 1940s. In 1977 the contents were auctioned, and the province declared the island protected. It is a National Historic Site, and the Passamaquoddy shell middens on it are a second one.',
  'Covenhoven, the 50-room sandstone-and-shingle mansion with its gift shop, the huge two-storey barn, the oceanfront bathhouse, the windmill, the gas plant, a garage and carriage house, the gardener\'s cottage with the remains of its vinery, a dairy and bunkhouse, and a late-18th-century stone farmhouse, among fields, carriage roads and forest. Interiors of the other buildings not checked.',
  'Drive across the sea floor to a railway king\'s summer house.',
  'The only way on is a gravel bar across the ocean floor at low tide, driven, cycled or walked in the hours round low water; the open hours change daily with the tides and are on its calendar. Overstay and you may wait six hours. Free parking at the ocean end of Bar Road on the mainland if you would rather walk the half kilometre. Tickets online or at the booth on the island. Dogs on a leash, not in the mansion; no smoking anywhere on the island.',
  [W('Ministers Island'),HP("Minister's Island National Historic Site of Canada",11961),L('Ministers Island: frequently asked questions','https://www.ministersisland.net/faq')],brick='?',hours='Crossing only round low tide; open hours change daily with the tides (its calendar)'),
# ---- The southeast: Memramcook and the Chignecto isthmus ----
N('monument-lefebvre','Monument-Lefebvre','Memramcook','nb','civic','standing',45.97913,-64.56673,'exact','open','walk','','1896–1897',
  'Built in 1896-97 on the campus of the Collège Saint-Joseph as a memorial to Father Camille Lefebvre, who died in 1895. He had founded the college, the first French-language institution in the Atlantic region to grant degrees, and was a leader of the Acadian revival of the late 19th century. The college closed in the 1970s and the building was kept as a memorial to Acadian culture; it is a National Historic Site.',
  'A symmetrical olive-sandstone building, rough-faced, with a hipped roof and conical pavilion roofs, a carved commemorative tablet, a date stone and memorial stained glass, on a hill over the valley. Inside, the theatre keeps its original volume and fittings: iron railing, original seating and wooden ceiling. Parks Canada\'s exhibit "Reflections of a Journey" tells the Acadians\' story.',
  'The Acadians\' monument to the priest who gave them a college.',
  'Parks Canada, on Rue Centrale. Open September to June Wednesday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 4 p.m. and Sunday 10 to 4; July and August daily 9 to 5 (its page). Mapped parking on Rue Centrale beside it (OpenStreetMap).',
  [HP('Monument-Lefebvre National Historic Site of Canada',11430),L('Parks Canada: Monument-Lefebvre','https://parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/nb/lefebvre')],th=[45.97929,-64.56766],thn='Lot on Rue Centrale (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no',hours='September to June: Wednesday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 4 p.m., Sunday 10 to 4. July and August: daily 9 to 5'),
N('fort-gaspareaux','Fort Gaspareaux','Port Elgin','nb','fort','traces',46.04289,-64.07075,'site','public','walk','forts','1751–1756',
  'French troops built this border post in 1751, on the orders of the Marquis de Jonquière, governor-general of New France, to keep the British out of the Chignecto isthmus and to supply the Acadian forts. On June 17, 1755 its 19 men under De Villeray surrendered to Colonel John Winslow; the British, who called it Fort Monckton, burned it in September 1756. The ground was farmed for a long time, but the site was always known; it was one of the first national historic sites (1920) and was dug by archaeologists in 1996.',
  'A small flat point of 1.23 hectares on the south side of the Gaspareaux estuary, behind a sea wall: the ditch and remnants of the curtain wall marking the fort\'s outline in the grass, and the graves of nine provincial soldiers killed in 1756 while holding it.',
  'A French border fort that the British burned within five years.',
  'Parks Canada, on Fort Monckton Road outside Port Elgin, 4.8 km from Baie Verte. Always open (its page). Mapped parking on Fort Monckton Road at the site (OpenStreetMap).',
  [W('Fort Gaspareaux'),HP('Fort Gaspareaux National Historic Site of Canada',7612),L('Parks Canada: Fort Gaspareaux','https://parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/nb/gaspareaux')],th=[46.04300,-64.07153],thn='Lot on Fort Monckton Road (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no',hours='Always open'),
# ---- The Miramichi ----
N('doak-house-doaktown','Doak House','Doaktown','nb','landmark','standing',46.5549,-66.1308,'site','open','drive','','before 1825–',
  'Robert Doak came from Ayrshire, Scotland and in 1825 bought this farm on the Southwest Miramichi from the Loyalist Betts family; besides farming he ran carding, grist, oat and saw mills, and the village took his name. His family lived here until 1979 (Wikipedia). The house, first a five-bay storey-and-a-half house with a central chimney built before 1825, grew to seven bays with a central hall in the mid 19th century. The province bought it with its furniture and runs it as a working farm museum.',
  'A storey-and-a-half clapboard house with close eaves, beaded corner boards and six-over-six windows, a wood fence along the road and an open field down to the large 19th-century barn. Inside, the central-hall plan with fireplace surrounds, staircase, built-in cupboards, chair rails and wainscot, rare horizontal sliding panelled shutters, grained six-panel doors, and the family\'s own 19th-century furniture. Costumed interpreters and craft demonstrations (Wikipedia).',
  'A Scottish settler\'s farm with his family\'s furniture still in it.',
  'Owned and run by the Province of New Brunswick (Register), at 386 Main Street (Route 8), with the Atlantic Salmon Museum as partner. Wikipedia says it opens from late June to early September, admission by donation; no page of the province\'s for it was found, so hours are not checked. No parking lot is mapped.',
  [W('Doak Historic Site'),HP('Doak House',5685)],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('beaubears-island','Boishébert and Beaubears Island','Miramichi','nb','town','traces',46.97291,-65.57235,'area','boat','boat','','1756–1760, 1790–',
  'Where the Northwest and Southwest Miramichi meet, Acadians fleeing the deportation took refuge under Charles Deschamps de Boishébert from 1756 to 1760, on Wilsons Point with Beaubears Island as its supply and lookout post; it was made a National Historic Site in 1930. In the early 19th century the island\'s south shore was a shipyard, with a two-storey stone house, a store and wharf, five slipways, a blacksmith\'s and joiner\'s shop, a moulding loft and houses for fifty workers, now a second National Historic Site.',
  'Nothing stands: on the island, archaeological traces of the shipyard and its buildings and a path older than 1840 down its middle, with 6 km of restored trails; on Wilsons Point (Enclosure Park) on the mainland, rare surface traces of the refugee camp, the remains of a cemetery and church site, a rectangular house site and a communal hearth (Register). An interpretation centre.',
  'Where Acadians hid from the deportation, and later built ships.',
  'Parks Canada; the Friends of Beaubears Island run the interpretation centre, and the island is reached by a ferry service. Open June 20 to September 6, 2026, daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., closed Canada Day (its page). Entry to the island is free; fees may be charged for the centre or the ferry. Where the ferry leaves from was not checked. The pin marks the island; the mapped lot on Enclosure Road serves Wilsons Point (OpenStreetMap).',
  [HP('Boishébert National Historic Site of Canada',1169),HP('Beaubears Island Shipbuilding National Historic Site of Canada',7650),L('Parks Canada: Boishébert and Beaubears Island Shipbuilding','https://parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/nb/boishebert')],th=[46.96423,-65.58251],thn='Lot on Enclosure Road, Wilsons Point (OpenStreetMap)',brick='?',hours='June 20 to September 6: daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., closed Canada Day'),
# ---- The Acadian Peninsula ----
N('village-historique-acadien','Village Historique Acadien','Bertrand','nb','town','standing',47.78909,-65.09758,'area','open','walk','','1770–1949',
  'An open-air museum put together in 1973-76 on the marshes of the Rivière du Nord near Caraquet, from Acadian buildings brought from across the province. The Register counts 38 historic buildings built between 1770 and 1939 and 9 reproductions; costumed interpreters portray Acadian families from 1770 to 1949. The marsh itself is worked with aboiteaux, the dykes, ditches and sluices by which Acadians turned salt marsh into farmland.',
  'About forty buildings along a 2.2 km path, houses, farm buildings, shops and a reproduction of the Moniteur Acadien printing office among them, with a collection of more than 8,000 artifacts, trade and farm demonstrations, and the working aboiteau system on the marsh. Interiors furnished; details not checked building by building.',
  'Two centuries of Acadian houses on one marsh.',
  'A provincial historic site, on Route 11 at New Bandon near Caraquet. 2026 season June 4 to September 26, 10 a.m. to 5:30 p.m. every day; a ticket covers two visits in the season; guided group tours by reservation (its pages). Allow four hours or more. OpenStreetMap maps a lot on Route 11 at the entrance.',
  [HP('Village Historique Acadien',6222),L('Village Historique Acadien: plan your visit','https://villagehistoriqueacadien.com/en/site/'),L('Village Historique Acadien: what you need to know','https://villagehistoriqueacadien.com/en/frequently-asked-questions/')],th=[47.78834,-65.09223],thn='Lot on Route 11 (OpenStreetMap)',brick='?',hours='June 4 to September 26: daily 10 a.m. to 5:30 p.m.'),
N('miscou-island-lighthouse','Miscou Island Lighthouse','Miscou','nb','light','standing',48.00927,-64.49296,'exact','open','drive','','1856–',
  'Built by the Province of New Brunswick in 1856 at Birch Point, the northeast tip of Miscou Island, to guard the entrance to Chaleur Bay; James Murray of Newcastle had the contract (Lighthouse Friends). It was raised from 22.5 to 24.3 metres in 1903 and in 1946 moved 61 metres inland away from the eroding shore. It is one of the oldest lights in the Gulf of St. Lawrence and one of the rarest wooden octagonal towers, its eight sides framed separately. The light is automated and still works.',
  'A tapering octagonal tower of heavy hand-hewn timber in white shingles with red trim, staggered four-over-four windows and a polygonal lantern behind a cast-iron rail; four storeys inside on straight stairs; a mid-20th-century foghorn building beside it. Lighthouse Friends says it still has its Fresnel lens.',
  'One of the oldest lights in the Gulf, moved back from the sea.',
  'On Route 113 at the northeast tip of Miscou Island. Lighthouse Friends (not the operator) says it reopened in 2010 after the province added a paved lot and washrooms, and that the tower is open in season from May to the October long weekend; no page of whoever runs it was read, so hours are not checked. Parking beside it (OpenStreetMap).',
  [HP('Miscou Island Lighthouse National Historic Site of Canada',16125),L('Lighthouse Friends: Miscou Island Lighthouse','https://www.lighthousefriends.com/light.asp?ID=1226')],th=[48.00918,-64.49371],thn='Lighthouse lot (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no',hours='?'),
]
